{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And with you shall be each man for a tribe, a man who is head of his ancestral house.</b> Since it says 'each man for a tribe,' why does it repeat and say 'a man who is head of his ancestral house'? And it is plausible to say that it resembles what the commentators wrote on the verse ״דָּן יָדִין עַמּוֹ כְּאַחַד שִׁבְטֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ (Genesis 49:16) — that he would not show favoritism toward his own tribe, who were called 'his people,' but rather Dan would judge them as one of the tribes of Israel, like one of the other tribes. So too these princes were upright, and each one was 'a man for a tribe': for the whole tribe he was like a man of valor to conduct his princehood over them with exaltedness. And not only for the whole tribe, those somewhat distant from him, but even regarding those who were 'head of his ancestral house' — those closest to him — to them as well he was a head, conducting princehood over them." "And the reason the princes were not mentioned in the first counting is that now He was dividing them into four standards, and each prince needed to know who exactly were those who marched and encamped under his standard. And Aaron was not mentioned in the first counting because its purpose was to know those who remained after the fall caused by the sin of the Golden Calf, for which Aaron was the cause; therefore he was not present there. But here, by the resting of the Divine Presence, which Aaron brought about through the offerings, he was present."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:39:30.841301243Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}